From b71707a76ae239c93990c0e473765de894a29fa4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 15:13:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Populate a static binding's pages only in base pages;
 tests take the advice as optional (#2546)

Review of the first commit found three things.

The tests asserted residency that only MADV_POPULATE_WRITE (Linux 5.14)
gives, though arena_fault_in calls it advice. Under a seccomp filter that
refuses advice 22 and 23 with EINVAL, as an older kernel does, test-link
failed 29 of its 30 populate checks, test-lanes 2 of its arena checks and
test-bind 1 of its 5 populate checks.
test-lanes, test-link and test-bind now probe a page first, as
c_check_deferred_test.c does, and check the faulted-in pages' residency only
where the kernel takes the advice; the pages that must not be resident, and
the arenas' peaks and lengths, are checked either way. All three pass under
the filter.

bind_populate faults in the most the members' joins can write: their rooms
(each atom's alignment less one beyond its size), every symbol of theirs
(those whose names the link has don't join) and every record (a discarded
COMDAT group's don't join). The directory, all that binding reads of a piece
before it checks it, holds nothing smaller. With huge pages that can fault
in a fresh 2 MB that nothing writes: with BUSTER_HUGE_PAGES=1 in the VM, a
static hello world with 1,458,176 bytes of data took 26 faults and added
8,332 KB to the peak in its `executable` stage populated, against 25 and
6,284 KB without (medians of 21 interleaved runs of one executable), where
a huge page's one fault for 2 MB left nothing to gain. Binding now
populates only where the process's arenas are base pages: arena_base_pages,
huge pages off for the process (a virtual machine, BUSTER_HUGE_PAGES=0, or
as it was started) or a busy host's arenas. With huge pages, as on the 9700X
under the lock, binding is as main's: that program's stage took 20 faults
and 76 KB with main's compiler and with this one, 22 and 2,088 KB with the
first commit's (medians of 11). In base pages the bounds cost 16 pages that
nothing writes for a static hello world (getrusage's faults in the stage,
which count populated pages: 336 against 320 without bind_populate).

bind_end's test checked only that the byte arena's peak reached its length
before the members plus their rooms, which populating after the joins also
passes. In base pages the peak must now be exactly that (709,517 bytes past
the length for main's members, against the 700,550 that bind_end's joins
push): populated after the joins, it is their end plus the rooms (1,410,053);
not at all, their end. Where the arenas may be huge pages it must be the bytes' end.
The direct test adds that case too. Eleven mutants of the new code (the
gate removed or inverted, populate dropped or moved after the joins or into
bind_end, and five wrong arena_base_pages) each fail test-bind or
test-lanes.

This doesn't meet #2546's done-when, which is measured on the 9700X: there
the arenas take huge pages, so the stage is as it was. It narrows the issue
for hosts whose arenas are base pages (virtual machines and busy hosts).

